  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 PM EST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nWe remain under an upper ridge with broad surface high pressure over \nthe region. Models keep this general pattern in place into at least \nthe first part of the weekend, as model trends have been to keep the \nweak low pressure system that tracks northeast near the E coast far \nenough off to our south and east for us to remain dry through at \nleast Saturday. Pleasantly warm afternoons will follow cool mornings \nover the next few days, with high temperatures by Friday and \nSaturday around 8 to 12 degrees above seasonal normals. \n\nOf note especially for the forestry folks, relative humidity values \nare very low this afternoon and will be again Thursday and likely \nFriday as well. Afternoon winds should remain on the light side. \n\nModels continue to show the upper ridge breaking down Sunday into \nMonday in response to a wave tracking through the Great Lakes and \nOhio Valley, and a weak cold front will move through our area around \nthe Sunday night/early Monday time frame. Currently there looks to \nbe limited moisture for the front to work with, and the low PoPs of \nthe NBM starting over portions of the area Sunday but primarily \nSunday night into Monday look reasonable. If any rain occurs with \nthis system it will likely be light. \n\nDrier and slightly cooler air will move in behind the front Monday \nand Tuesday, with temperatures back down closer to seasonal normals. \nModels are in very poor agreement by the end of the period, but the \nensemble approach yields a chance of rain moving back into our area \nby Wednesday.",
